{{also|Phlogiston}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|phlogiston theory}}

===Etymology===
Coined by [[w:Georg Ernst Stahl|Stahl]] in 1702, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|φλογιστόν|tr=phlogiston|lang=grc}}, neuter of {{term|φλογιστός||inflammable|tr=phlogistos|lang=grc}}, from {{term|φλογίζω||to set fire to|tr=phlogizō|lang=grc}}, from {{term|φλόξ||flame|tr=phloks|lang=grc}}. {{attention|grc}}<!-- why? -->

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/flə(ʊ)ˈdʒɪstɒn/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{context|chemistry|historical|lang=en}} The [[hypothetical]] [[fiery]] [[principle]] formerly assumed to be a necessary [[constituent]] of [[combustible]] bodies and to be given up by them in [[burning]].

====Related terms====
* [[dephlogisticated air]]

